{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (release 2.08, the stable release): ferritins, ribonucleotide reductase R2-like proteins, and manganese catalases are all placed in superfamily a.25.1 Ferritin-like (sunid 47240). That superfamily sits in fold a.25 Ferritin-like (sunid 47239) under class a, All alpha proteins (sunid 46456). Its three child families are a.25.1.1 Ferritin (sunid 47241), a.25.1.2 Ribonucleotide reductase-like (sunid 47253; retrieved protein node 'Ribonucleotide reductase R2', sunid 47257), and a.25.1.3 Manganese catalase (T-catalase) (sunid 100951; retrieved protein node sunid 47263). Ferritin protein nodes returned by search ((Apo)ferritin, Dodecameric ferritin homolog, Non-hem ferritin) are all a.25.1.1. The superfamily annotation comment reads 'contains bimetal-ion centre in the middle of the bundle'. Homology responses for a.25.1, a.25.1.1, a.25.1.2 and a.25.1.3 show homology_implied_by_level=true with no warnings. The Mn catalase family carries an automatic Pfam mapping (PF05067). The RNR R1 subunit domains are classified outside this superfamily (a.98.1.1 N-terminal, c.7.1.2 C-terminal), so R1 is not a member of a.25.1.

Interpretation: SCOPe makes a positive homology assertion only at the superfamily level. Ferritin, R2-like and Mn catalase families therefore share a probable common ancestor within a.25.1. Their placement in three different families means SCOPe does not describe them as close relatives of one another; the relationship is a distant superfamily-level one. The fold a.25 level is structural similarity (a shared Ferritin-like helical-bundle architecture, from background knowledge rather than a retrieved SCOPe record). The chemistries differ: ferritin performs ferroxidation and iron-core mineralization, R2 uses a diiron-tyrosyl radical to initiate ribonucleotide reduction, and Mn catalase dismutates hydrogen peroxide with a dimanganese site. These are literature facts that the API did not return. They show functional divergence within one superfamily, which SCOPe permits. They do not overturn the superfamily-level homology assertion, and the superfamily placement itself does not tell you which reaction, metal, or substrate any member uses.

